WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.040
Scientists identified a previously unknown drainage pathway at the back of the eye that carries fluid and waste into the lymphatic system. This is Planetarian Daily, Episode 210. Researchers at the University of British Columbia and the University of Toronto identified the posterior-ocular lymphatic outflow pathway in mice. The pathway drains fluid and metabolic waste from the retina into the body's lymphatic network. The retina is one of the most metabolically active parts of the body.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.380
constantly generating byproducts that need to be cleared.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.540
Dr. Niru Gupta, professor and head of UBC's Department of Ophthalmology and Visual Sciences,

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.300
said the discovery helps explain how the eye flushes this waste.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.280
The system was demonstrated through direct tracing of outflow roots in the animal model.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.380
The finding challenges long-standing assumptions about how the eye functions.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.880
The team named the root, the posterior ocular lymphatic outflow, or polo pathway.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:01.240
pathway. The newly identified posterior ocular lymphatic outflow pathway offers a concrete

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:06.340
root for studying how metabolic waste leaves the retina. Earlier models treated the back

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:11.580
of the eye as largely isolated from lymphatic clearance, which left unexplained how fluid

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:17.920
and inflammatory material accumulated in glaucoma and macular degeneration. Direct tracing in mice

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.820
now shows fluid moving from retinal tissue into lymphatic vessels that connect to systemic

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:28.180
drainage. This changes the experimental questions researchers can ask about pressure regulation

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:33.500
and waste build-up. Clinicians may eventually test whether enhancing flow along this route

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:39.080
alters disease progression in specific patient groups. One open measurement is whether the pathway

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:44.960
operates at different rates across age or disease states and humans. A year-long randomized trial

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:51.080
at the University of Utah, New York University, and Stanford University tested foot-angle retraining

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:52.960
in people with knee osteoarthritis.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:58.260
Participants who adopted a personalized walking angle reported pain reductions comparable

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.520
to medication effects.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.360
MRI scans showed slower loss of cartilage integrity in the adjusted group.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.780
The approach alters mechanical loading on the knee without drugs or surgery.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.960
The trial was the first placebo controlled study of gait retraining maintained over 12

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:14.340
months.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.900
Researchers measured both pain scores and imaging markers of joint health.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.300
The method changes how the foot strikes the ground to shift pressure away from damaged

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:24.220
cartilage areas.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:29.340
Most people assume knee arthritis pain stems only from cartilage loss that medication

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.600
or surgery must address, yet the trial shows a simple shift in foot position during walking

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.080
measurably reduces load on damaged areas.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.760
The knee joint receives different forces depending on the angle at which the foot contacts

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.060
the ground with each step.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.220
One practical detail from the year-long study is that participants maintain the adjusted

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:53.440
gate without ongoing supervision after initial training. When the foot angle moves pressure

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.200
away from the inner knee compartment, cartilage experiences less compressive stress over

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.420
thousands of daily steps. Future imaging could confirm whether this mechanical change slows

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:08.860
structural decline beyond the one-year mark already measured. People with knee osteoarthritis

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:13.660
can consult a physical therapist to test a personalized foot angle during walking assessments.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:19.660
A team at TU Ween constructed the first nuclear clock that maintains stability without continuous

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.300
reference to an atomic clock.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.340
The device uses transitions between nuclear energy states and thorium atoms triggered

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:27.220
by laser light.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:31.940
Researchers showed steady operation for more than 24 hours in the initial tests.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:37.560
The small energy gap between thorium nuclear states allows laser control that is not feasible

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.940
with most other nuclei.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:45.500
I. Earlier work in 2024 established the laser excitation of thorium nuclei. The clock could

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:50.200
eventually exceed the precision of current optical atomic clocks for metrology applications.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:55.300
The breakthrough relies on thorium's unusual nuclear property that permits laser-driven

00:03:55.300 --> 00:04:01.460
transitions. Engineers at NYU Abu Dhabi and Cleveland Clinic Abu Dhabi developed an injectable

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:06.800
device roughly the size of a seed. The implant delivers programmable electrical stimulation

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.380
near-targeted nerves without batteries or wires. Power reaches the device wirelessly from outside

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:18.740
the body. Placement can be guided and verified with ultrasound or CT imaging. Early tests showed

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:24.360
the device can alter nerve firing patterns in real time. The work was published in science advances.

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:29.600
The technology aims to treat chronic pain and movement disorders through precise, adjustable

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:36.700
stimulation. The Neoprism CRC trial gave Pembrolysmab for up to nine weeks before surgery to 32

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:42.300
patients with a specific genetic subtype of stage two or three bowel cancer. After nearly

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:47.900
three years of follow-up, no patient experienced recurrence. The result held even for participants

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.520
who retained small traces of disease after treatment. The regimen replaced standard post-operative

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:59.520
chemotherapy in this group. The trial focused on mismatched repair deficient tumors. Outcomes were

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.460
tracked through imaging and clinical monitoring. The approach targets tumors with high mutation

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:07.720
burdens that respond strongly to immune checkpoint blockade.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:14.280
Texas A&amp;M researchers exposed mice to polyethylene microplastics and observed increased markers

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.380
of fatty liver disease.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.860
The effect occurred even without an added high fat diet.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.860
When combined with a diet high in fat, fructose, and cholesterol, liver damage indicators rose

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:25.360
further.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:30.460
Polyethylene accounts for roughly one-third of global plastic production and appears in

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:31.960
food packaging and containers.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:37.840
doctors. Dr. Adi Joshi stated that these particles affect the liver's natural defense and repair mechanisms.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.680
The study measured fat accumulation inside liver cells as the primary outcome.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:48.560
The findings suggest environmental pollutants and poor diet may reinforce each other's effects on the liver.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:54.220
Researchers at the University of Cologne, University of Oxford, University of Liège,

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:59.700
and Cape Breton University produced birch tar using methods inferred from Neanderthal sites.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:03.960
Every sample inhibited growth of bacteria that caused wound infections.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:09.120
The material was previously understood mainly as an adhesive for halfting stone tools.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:14.260
The team tested the TAR directly on relevant bacterial strains in laboratory assays.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:16.960
The findings appear in PLOS1.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:22.560
The work suggests Neanderthals may have recognized additional uses for the substance beyond tool

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:23.080
construction.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.360
The TAR was obtained by heating birch bark in controlled conditions that match archeological

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:28.840
evidence.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:35.260
palaeontologists described Musango, Matusodona, and Sis from fossils recovered along the Niamasango

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:43.040
River. The sub-adult specimen measured approximately 4.5 meters long and weighed 220 kg. Bone growth

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:48.820
marks indicated it was still growing at death. The skeleton shows bipedal posture and belongs to the

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:54.500
group that later produced long neck giants. The fine demonstrates that Zimbabwean dinosaur faunas

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:59.660
contained species distinct from those in South Africa. Environmental differences are suspected

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:05.560
as the driver of regional variation. The incomplete skeleton lacked a skull, leaving diet uncertain,

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:11.320
though relatives were largely herbivorous. Looking back over the past week, three threads stand out.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:16.780
Optogenetics earned the Nobel Prize in Medicine, opening fresh questions about how light-controlled

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:22.860
neurons might translate into therapies. A collar-mounted sensor began recording the precise particle levels

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:28.160
wildlife experience during wildfire smoke, adding real-time data to exposure studies.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:32.880
Experiments showed that aspen trees retained chemical memories of past droughts in their

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:38.080
leaves for at least three years, while conflict continues to reduce crop land area in affected

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.000
regions long after fighting ends.

